\title{Advanced parameter guide for GeneTrackR}
\description{
This help topic summarizes frequently used advanced parameters that are shared
by several GeneTrackR functions. It is intended as a quick reference for
custom colors, highlighted intervals, signal transformations, sample grouping,
and strand handling.
}
\section{Gene model colors}{

Gene model functions such as \code{\link[=plot_gene]{plot_gene()}}, \code{\link[=plot_transcript]{plot_transcript()}}, and
\code{\link[=plot_region]{plot_region()}} use \code{gene_palette} and \code{gene_colors} for exon/CDS/UTR fills.
\code{gene_palette} accepts any palette from \code{RColorBrewer::brewer.pal.info}. If
the number of discrete groups exceeds the palette limit, colors are
interpolated automatically.

\code{gene_colors} can be either unnamed or named. For gene models, the most stable
named form is:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{gene_colors = c(
  CDS = "#33a02c",
  UTR = "#b2df8a",
  exon = "#fb9a99"
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Unnamed colors are matched to the observed discrete groups in plotting order.
}

\section{Highlight intervals}{

\code{highlight} must be a data frame with at least \code{start} and \code{end} columns:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{highlight = data.frame(start = 105000, end = 106000)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

In genomic plots, \code{start} and \code{end} are genomic coordinates. In transcript
coordinate plots, they are spliced transcript coordinates.
}

\section{Signal colors}{

Signal plotting functions use \code{signal_palette}, \code{signal_palette_direction}, \code{signal_colors},
\code{sample_groups}, and \code{signal_color_by}.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{signal_palette = "YlGnBu"
signal_palette_direction = -1
signal_colors = c(sampleA = "#2166AC", sampleB = "#B2182B")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Group-level coloring is controlled by a named vector or a two-column data
frame:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{sample_groups = c(KO1 = "KO", KO2 = "KO", WT1 = "WT", WT2 = "WT")
signal_color_by = "group"
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Signal y-axis}{

Signal plots support transformed values and per-sample or shared y-axis
ranges:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{signal_transform = "sqrt"  # none, log2, log10, or sqrt
signal_y_scale = "free"   # free or fixed
signal_y_ticks = "range"  # range or pretty
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{signal_y_ticks = "range"} displays integer y-axis limits only.
}

\section{Strand handling}{

Standard bigWig and wig tracks are unstranded. For such tracks,
\code{strand_policy = "ignore_unstranded"} lets \code{strand = "+"} or \code{"-"} return the
same unstranded signal. Use \code{strand_policy = "strict"} when you only want
records with explicit strand information, such as plus/minus bedGraph tracks.
}

